description:
And eliminate double dispatch for `dbExistsTable()` and friends

```R
setMethod(
  "dbExistsTable", c("OdbcConnection"),
  function(conn, name, ...) {
    
    if (is(name, "SQL") || inherits(x, "AsIs")) {
      # converts to an Id object
      name <- dbUnquoteIdentifier(conn, name)[[1]]
    }
     
    if (is(name, "Id")) {
      df <- odbcConnectionTables(
        conn,
        name = id_field(name, "table"),
        catalog_name = id_field(name, "catalog"),
        schema_name = id_field(name, "schema")
      )
    } else if (is.character(name) && length(name) == 1) {
      df <- odbcConnectionTables(conn, name = name, ..., exact = TRUE)
    } else {
      abort("`name` must be a string, Id(), SQL() object.")
    }
    
    NROW(df) > 0
  }
)
```

Need to check if we need `c("OdbcConnection", "ANY")` in the signature to avoid ambiguous dispatch, and if we can make `dbWriteTable()` work the same way.

### Issue Description and Expected Result

Uploading a large (~100MB) table to azure SQL results in a timeout from Azure if the whole table is attempted at once.

If I attempt to break the table into chunks and loop through the chunks, the `dbAppendTable` function does not appear to wait for the current chunk to finish appending before attempting to start the next chunk.  Moreover, it seems that `dbAppendTable` attempts to send each row individually - this is convenient for, say, doing uniqueness checks on each row, but it results in *incredibly* slow performance (about 10 rows per second).  I have verified that the bottleneck is not on the side of the Azure database (it is only hitting ~40% capacity during an attempted upload).

I have attempted wrapping the chunk appends in transactions, to no avail.

### Database

Azure SQL

### Reproducible Example

This is not exactly reproducible, as I have no way of easily providing the files here.  Regardless, this gives the gist of what I'm trying to do:

```R
for (i in seq(from=1, to=nrow(data), by=500)) {
  tryCatch({
    dbWithTransaction(db, {
      dbAppendTable(db, "table_name", data[i:i+499, ])
    })
  }, error = function(e) {
    errs <- errs %>% add_row(i = i,
                             err = toString(e),
                             size = 500)
    print(e)
  })
}
```

As written, this results in records being appended to the table until the for loop finishes attempting each chunk, at which point it merely stops at whatever row it happened to reach before the loop finished.  The loop appears to just spin happily without waiting for any of the transactions to finish.

### Issue Description and Expected Result
dbConnect(odbc::odbc(), .connection_string = ...) works fine except for one issue:
If there is a wrong or misspelled Server in the connection string, R will state "Keine Rückmeldung" ("No response") and, well, won't respond anymore. I'm using Rx64 3.4.1.

### Database
SQL Server 2014

### Reproducible Example


```r
library(DBI)

cs <- "Driver={SQL Server};
        Server=XYZ;
        Database=testDB;
        UID=;
        Pwd=;
        trusted_connection=TRUE;" 

con <- dbConnect(odbc::odbc(), .connection_string = cs)
```
